 { Material is equal but Black has a clear advantage thanks to his more active pieces (in particular, the enemy knight is very badly placed on a4) and superior pawn structure. Kasparov proceeds with the most obvious plan in the position. } 1... { -KEY- } h5 $1 { Passed pawns should move forward, as the old saying goes. } 2. Rc5 Ng2+ { This intermediate check clears the f-file for the rook on f7, so that a later f2-f3 can be met by ...Rxf3. } 3. Ke2 h4 $1 { Forward! The idea of enhancing a passed pawn at the cost of material is quite typical not only in the endgame, but in the middlegame as well. } 4. Rxe5+ Kg4 5. Re8 { Opting for a rear attack, usually a good option in the battle against a passed pawn, but here Black will be able to neutralize it with simple means. } ( { a) Nevertheless, after } 5. Kf1 Nf4 6. Rf5 Rd7 $1 ) 
 ( { b) or } 5. f3 $5 Rxf3 6. Rg5 Kxg5 7. Kxf3 Ne1 8. Kf2 Nd3 9. Kf3 Ne5 $1 10. Kg2 Kg4 { White is lost anyway; e.g. } 11. Nc5 h3 12. Kf2 ( { or } 12. Kh2 Kh4 ) 
Kh4 13. Ne6 Nd3 14. Kf3 Ne1 15. Kf2 h2 { - Kasparov. } ) 
 ( { c) As for } 5. Rxa5 { after } h3 6. f3 Kh4 7. Kf2 Nf4 8. Ra8 Rh7 $1 9. Kg1 Rg7 10. Kh1 Nd3 { heading for f2, Black is again winning - Krasenkow. } ) 
Nf4+ 6. Ke3 h3 7. f3+ ( { Or } 7. Rg8 Kh4 { with the decisive threat of ...Nh5, building a 'bridge'. } ) 
Kh4 ( { In view of } 7... Kh4 8. Rh8 Nh5 9. Kf2 Rg7 $1 { followed by ...h3-h2, White resigned. } ) 

 0-1

