 { A passed pawn has a kind of dual nature - it can be incredibly strong, exhausting all the enemy resources, but it can also turn into a weakness if insufficiently protected.Black has just played ...Kg8-f7 in order to approach the c7-pawn, a plan that White must stop by any means. } 1. { -KEY- } Rc6 $1 Ke7 2. Nc5 $1 { An important triumph for White - the opponent's king cannot get any closer to the passed pawn. } e5 3. h5 { By this move Vallejo signals the plan of a new 'front' on the kingside which, if successful, is bound to exhaust Black's defences. } e4 4. Kf1 ( { The immediate } 4. g4 { was also fine. Please notice that Black has difficulties moving at this point, which makes a good occasion for a useful move like } 4. Kf1 { , bringing the king a step closer to Black's passed pawn. } ) 
Bd4 5. g4 $1 ( { Getting on with the plan. Another tempting option was } 5. Na6 { when } Kd7 { can be met by } 6. Nb8 { but Vallejo's solution is more clear-cut. } ) 
Bxc5 6. Rxc5 Kd6 7. Rc1 Ke5 ( { Both } 7... Rxc7 8. Rxc7 Kxc7 9. g5 ) 
 ( { and } 7... d4 8. g5 hxg5 9. h6 d3 10. Ke1 $1 Kd5 11. h7 { win for White - Finkel. } ) 
8. g5 $1 hxg5 9. Rc6 $1 ( { Before pushing the h-pawn, White cuts off the enemy king from the sixth rank. } 9. h6 { would also work, e.g. } Kf6 10. Ke2 d4 11. Rh1 $1 Rxc7 12. h7 Rc2 13. Kd1 { etc. - hobbsey. } ) 
d4 10. h6 Kd5 11. Rc1 Kd6 12. h7 Kd7 { By now virtually any move wins for White, since Black will end up in zugzwang. } 13. Rc4 ( { In view of lines like } 13. Rc4 d3 14. Rd4 Ke7 15. Rd8 { Black resigned. } ) 

 1-0

