 { Material is equal but White has a magnificent passed pawn; besides, the bishop tends to outperform the knight in open positions. } 1. { -KEY- } a6 { The further this pawn gets, the stronger. } ( { Other moves are less convincing; e.g. } 1. Bc4 $6 Nc7 2. a6 $2 Nxa6 3. Bxa6 g5 $1 { simplifying to a theoretical draw. } ) 
g5 2. a7 Nc7 3. Kf2 { Heading for the queenside to assist the passed pawn. } ( { Of course } 3. Bg2 $6 gxf4 4. a8=Q $2 Nxa8 5. Bxa8 { also had to be avoided. } ) 
gxf4 4. Kf3 Kg5 5. Bc4 { Due to the knight's inability to lose tempi, Black will soon end up in zugzwang. } Na8 6. Bd5 Nc7 7. Bc6 Kg6 ( 7... h6 8. Bb7 { doesn't change anything. } ) 
8. Kxf4 Kf6 9. Bb7 h6 10. Ke3 $1 { Heading for b6, a plan that Black can only prevent temporarily. } Ke5 11. Kd3 Kd6 12. Kc4 Kd7 ( 12... f4 13. Bf3 { is similar. } ) 
13. Kc5 Kd8 14. Bc6 Ke7 15. Kb6 Kd8 16. Kb7 f4 17. Be4 $1 { Losing a tempo. } Kd7 18. Bf3 Kd8 19. Bc6 { Finding himself in a fatal zugzwang, Black resigned. } 
 1-0

