 { As we discussed in Krush-Akopian, the queen is an excellent companion of the passed pawn, something that converts the latter into the key factor of many queen endings. } 1. { -KEY- } a5 $1 { White pins all his hopes on the passed pawns. } d4 2. Qb7 $1 Qxf2+ ( { Or } 2... d3 3. Qxf7 Ke4 4. b6 { with an easy win. } ) 
3. Qg2 Qe3 4. b6 $1 ( { But not } 4. a6 $2 d3 { and the pawns can't advance any further. } ) 
Qb3 $2 { This attempt to control the passed pawns fails fatally to White's next move. } ( { The brave } 4... d3 $1 5. b7 d2 { had to be tried; e.g. } 6. b8=Q $11 ( { or } 5... Qb3 5. Qf1 $6 Kg4 $1 7. b8=Q $11 Qxg3 8. Kh1 Qh4 { with a perpetual; } ) 
d1=Q $11 7. Qh3 Kf6 8. Qh4 g5 $5 9. hxg6 Qg5 10. Qb6 Kg7 { and Black is still alive. } ) 
5. a6 $1 { An elegant sacrifice which secures the promotion of the a-pawn. } Qxb6 6. Qb7 Qa5 7. a7 ( { In view of } 7. a7 Qa2 8. Kh3 { Black resigned. } ) 

