 { Of course a passed pawn doesn't mean an automatic win. In the example below, Black, despite being a pawn down, managed to save himself by launching a direct attack on the d5-pawn. } 1... { -KEY- } Kf6 $1 { The king should approach the pawn while it is insufficiently protected, and this is the right route. } ( { In contrast, } 1... Kf8 $6 { permits } 2. Ne5 $1 Bc7 3. f4 { with Kf3-e4 coming up. } ) 
2. Kf1 ( { The attempt to create a 'barrier' (more on this topic in Chapter Four) by } 2. Nd2 Bc7 3. Nc4 { fails to } b5 ) 
Ke7 3. Nd2 ( { Or } 3. Ke2 Kd6 4. Ng5 f5 $1 { - Speelman. } ) 
Bc7 $1 ( { Some prophylaxis is recommended. In contrast, the immediate } 3... Kd6 $6 { allows } 4. Nc4 Kc5 5. Ne5 ( 4... Bc7 4. d6 { is tempting, but after } f6 $1 { , denying Nc4-e5 while preparing ...Bd8 and ...b7-b5, White has difficulties making progress, as noticed by KweenZaekrefais. } ) 
Kxd5 6. Nxf7 { , with greater practical chances for victory than in the game, since one more pair of pawns stays on the board. } ) 
4. Ne4 ( { Or } 4. Nc4 b5 5. Ne3 Kd6 { followed by ...Bb6, and the d-pawn falls anyway. } ) 
f5 $1 { Preventing 5.g5 with a decisive barrier. Besides, pawn exchanges tend to help the defender. } 5. gxf5 gxf5 6. Nc5 Kd6 $1 7. Nxb7+ Kxd5 { White remains a pawn up, but he is now forced to weaken his pawn structure in order to save the badly placed knight. } 8. b4 Kc4 $1 9. Nc5 ( { Or } 9. a3 Kb3 10. Nc5 Kxa3 11. Nxa6 Bb6 12. Ke2 Ka4 { followed by ...Kb5 - Speelman. } ) 
Bb6 $1 ( { A smart way of forcing the elimination of White's queenside pawns. In contrast, } 9... a5 $6 10. Ne6 $1 Bb6 11. bxa5 Bxa5 12. Ke2 { would unnecessarily prolong the battle, while } Kxb4 $4 { obviously failed to } 10. Nxa6 ) 
 ( { A similar case is } 9... Bd6 $6 { , since after } 10. Nxa6 Kb5 11. Nc5 Kxb4 12. Ne6 { heading for g7, the battle would be prolonged. No wonder Korchnoi opted for the more precise } Bb6 { in order to be able to take f2 at this very point. } ) 
10. Nxa6 Kb5 11. Nc5 Kxb4 12. Nb3 Ka3 ( { Draw agreed, in view of } 12... Ka3 13. Nc1 Kb2 ) 

 1/2-1/2

