 ( { In a sharp Grunfeld battle, Black, having just played } 22. Rb7 b6 { , seems to be coming out fine from the complications. However, Kramnik has a strong reply prepared. } ) 
23. { -KEY- } d6 $1 ( { Thanks to the tactical detail } 23. d6 exd6 24. Bxb6 { White manages to convert his d-pawn into a magnificent passed pawn. } ) 
Rac8 ( { After } 23... bxa5 24. dxe7 Rfe8 25. Bxa8 Rxa8 26. Rd7 { followed by a king march via f1-e2-d3-c4, White should be winning. } ) 
24. d7 Rcd8 { This leads to a very passive game. } ( { Perhaps } 24... bxa5 $5 25. dxc8=Q $11 Rxc8 { was a lesser evil; e.g. } 26. Bd4 Bxd4 27. Rxd4 { with a clear, but not decisive advantage - Yusupov. } ) 
25. Bxe7 $1 Bxe7 ( { Obviously, } 25... bxa5 $2 { failed to } 26. Bxf6 { - Ftacnik. } ) 
26. Rxa7 { Despite material equality and opposite-coloured bishops, White has a huge edge thanks to his far advanced passed pawn. } Rb8 { Assigning the blockading task to the bishop means that Black will be left without active pieces; } ( { but after } 26... Bc5 27. Bc6 { preparing Re1-e8, Ra8 etc, it is hard to see any defence for him either. } ) 
27. Re1 Bd8 28. Re8 $1 { Immobilizing most of the enemy pieces. } b5 29. Ra8 Rxa8 30. Bxa8 b4 31. Bd5 Kg7 32. Kf1 ( { Black resigned, already aware that he can't prevent a decisive transfer of the white king to c6; } 32. Kf1 f5 33. Ke2 Bc7 34. Kd3 Bd8 35. Kc4 Ba5 36. Kb5 Bc7 37. Kxb4 { followed by Kb5-c6-b7-c8. } ) 

