 ( { White has just played Rb4-e4, perhaps awaiting } 1... Qd6 { when } 2. Bf4 e5 3. Rc1 $1 { would let him challenge the active rook on c2. However, Hansen comes up with a better idea. } ) 
 { -KEY- } Qd5 $1 { Sacrificing the e-pawn in order to speed up the inherent plan in the position: the creation of a powerful passed pawn on the a-file. } ( { Rather than } 1... Qd6 2. Bf4 e5 3. Rc1 $1 ) 
2. Rxe7 b5 3. Qe4 $6 ( { With hindsight, White should have contented himself with equality after } 3. Qa6 Rxe2 4. Rb7 Qxb3 5. Rxb5 ) 
Qxe4 4. dxe4 ( { On } 4. Rxe4 { Black could react as in the game. } ) 
a5 $1 { Simple and strong; the e2-pawn can wait. Now there is no evident defence against the advance of the a-pawn. } 5. Rb7 a4 6. Rxb5 a3 { As often happens, an advanced passed pawn annuls any other strategic factors. } 7. e5 f6 $1 { Again the e2-pawn can wait - what matters is the rapid re-inclusion of the bishop in the battle. } 8. Bd4 { Intending a blockade with the bishop on a1, but as the remainder of the game will show us, this idea won't save White in the end. } ( { a) Then again, after } 8. f4 fxe5 9. fxe5 Rxe2 10. Bd4 a2 { preparing ...Rd8; } ) 
 ( { b) or } 8. Rc5 Rxe2 9. b4 a2 10. Ra5 Rxa5 11. bxa5 Rb2 $1 12. a6 Rb4 { heading for a4, White is also doomed. } ) 
fxe5 9. Ba1 ( { Or } 9. Bxe5 $2 Bxe5 10. Rxe5 a2 11. Ra1 Rb2 { etc. } ) 
Rxe2 { Black has finally re-established the material balance, with a huge advantage thanks to his far-advanced passed pawn. } 10. b4 a2 11. Rb7 Rf8 12. Kg2 Bh8 13. Ra7 e4 $1 { Not just in order to swap the defender on a1, but also with the idea of exhausting White's first rank defence by ...e4-e3. } 14. Bxh8 Kxh8 15. b5 ( { White now loses by one tempo; but on other moves, such as } 15. Ra3 { there follows } e3 $1 { anyway. } ) 
e3 16. b6 Rfxf2+ 17. Rxf2 Rxf2+ 18. Kh3 e2 19. b7 Rf8 20. Ra8 e1=Q 21. Rxf8+ Kg7 22. b8=Q ( { Or } 22. Rg8 Kh6 $1 23. Rh8 Kg5 { etc. } ) 
Qe6+ ( { In view of } 22... Qe6 23. Kg2 a1=Q $11 { with a trivial win, White resigned. } ) 

 0-1

